caymanslm: hide runtime-injected SELinux types from unprivileged app probes

Duck Detector's DANGER card ("Enforcing with KSU context materialized") is
driven by one fact leaking through the SELinux userspace query surface:
KernelSU injects its own types (ksu, ksu_file) into the live policydb after the
base policy has loaded, and module sepolicy.rule files can inject more (e.g.
xposed_data). On a stock kernel those labels do not exist, so a context->SID
conversion returns -EINVAL; here they resolve. The detector reads that
difference three ways, all from an ordinary app:

  - /proc/self/attr/current write   -> selinux_setprocattr(): a candidate
    "u:r:ksu:s0" resolves and the transition is denied (EPERM) instead of the
    stock EINVAL. Classified as "KernelSU context materialized".
  - /sys/fs/selinux/context write   -> sel_write_context(): the context
    validity oracle accepts "u:r:ksu:s0" / "u:object_r:ksu_file:s0".
  - /sys/fs/selinux/access write    -> sel_write_access(): edges naming a
    ksu/ksu_file/xposed_data type report allowed.

Rather than match type-name strings (fragile, and it would not survive a rename
of the ksu domain), key off a structural invariant: a type whose value exceeds
the type count captured the instant the base policy finished loading was
injected at runtime. selinux_stock_type_ceiling records that count in
security_load_policy() (both the first-load and the reload path, so a genuine
policy reload re-arms it before KernelSU re-injects).
selinux_sid_is_hidden_from_caller() maps a SID to its type value and reports it
hidden when it is above the ceiling.

WHO it hides from is the crucial part, and the reason for v2 of this patch. The
gate must fire ONLY for sandboxed apps -- uid >= 10000 with seccomp installed,
the same heuristic KernelSU's own selinux_hide feature uses. It must NOT fire
for the root stack's uid-0 machinery. In particular the ReZygisk monitor/daemon,
zygote and system_server legitimately resolve injected types to set up Zygisk
injection: rezygisk's sepolicy.rule adds "type zygisk_file" and needs
"allow zygote ksu {dir search, file read}" (its comment notes "KernelSU renamed
su context to ksu") to setns into the ksu mount namespace. An earlier revision
gated on "not the ksu domain", which also hid these injected types from zygote
and the uid-0 monitor -- zygiskd then failed to start and no app got injected
(verified: on the old kernel zygiskd ran; with the over-broad gate it did not,
same boot mode). The uid>=10000+seccomp gate spares all of that while still
covering a detector, which always probes from an app.

The three query paths return -EINVAL for a hidden SID, exactly as a stock
kernel does for a context whose type does not exist. No legitimate app validates
or queries a ksu/ksu_file/xposed_data label, so nothing else is affected, and
enforcement itself is untouched -- this only sanitises what the query interfaces
disclose to apps.

Does not touch the "dirty edge" residue on standard domains (e.g.
zygote -> adb_data_file:dir search), which involves no injected type; that is
secondary and mostly LineageOS/module policy, not this stack.

Sorts last in patches/kernel/ so it applies after every other patch that edits
security/selinux/{hooks,selinuxfs}.c and ss/services.c. Guarded by
CONFIG_KSU_SUSFS, which is already stripped from /proc/config.gz by
caymanslm-sanitized-ikconfig.patch, so it discloses no new config symbol.

